REST API Design

 1. Core principles of REST architecture and their application to API design REST (Representational State Transfer) principles include: - Stateless: Each request contains all necessary information; no server-side session state. - Client-Server: Separation of concerns between client (UI) and server (data/logic). - Uniform Interface: Standardized methods (GET, POST, etc.), resource-based URLs, and HATEOAS. - Resource-Based: Resources (e.g., "books") are identified by URLs and manipulated via HTTP methods. - Layered System: APIs can use intermediaries (e.g., proxies) without affecting client interaction. - Cacheable: Responses can be cached to improve performance. These principles ensure scalability, simplicity, and interoperability in API design.

 2. Difference between GET, POST, PUT, DELETE HTTP methods - GET: Retrieves a resource (e.g., list books). Idempotent, safe, and cacheable. - POST: Creates a new resource (e.g., add a book). Not idempotent. - PUT: Updates an existing resource or creates it if it doesn’t exist (e.g., update a book). Idempotent. - DELETE: Removes a resource (e.g., delete a book). Idempotent. Use GET for fetching, POST for creation, PUT for updates, and DELETE for deletion.

 3. Structuring RESTful endpoints for a resource like "books" or "todos" - Use resource-based URLs with clear hierarchy: - GET /books: List all books. - GET /books/:id: Get a specific book by ID. - POST /books: Create a new book. - PUT /books/:id: Update a book by ID. - DELETE /books/:id: Delete a book by ID. - For todos, follow the same pattern (e.g., GET /todos, POST /todos, etc.). - Use query parameters for filtering or pagination (e.g., GET /books?author=John&limit=10). --- ### MVC Architecture Pattern 1. What is the MVC pattern and how does it help organize code? MVC (Model-View-Controller) is a design pattern that separates application logic into three components: - Model: Manages data and business logic. - View: Handles the user interface (not typically used in APIs). - Controller: Acts as an intermediary between Model and View, handling user input.
